IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Insurance Contracts Act 1984 (Cth), with the Insurance Contracts Regulations 2017 that set its rates and standard cover --
-- what an insured must tell the insurer before the contract (the duty to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation
-- for a consumer insurance contract, s 20B; the duty of disclosure for any other contract, s 21), the duty of the utmost good
-- faith both ways (ss 13, 14), what the insurer may do about a relevant failure (ss 27AA, 28, 29, 30, 32), when it cannot
-- refuse a claim because of something done after the contract (s 54), and the claim itself (ss 40(3), 56, 57).
--
-- SEAT: the insured -- and, for cover inside a superannuation fund, the life insured, who is not the insured (the trustee is).
-- Every decision is phrased as that person would ask it: "Must I disclose this matter?", "Is my statement a misrepresentation?",
-- "What may the insurer do because of my relevant failure?", "May the insurer refuse my claim because of what happened after
-- the contract?". Where a provision addresses only the insurer (ss 22, 28, 29, 54, 57, 58, 59), the decision says what it
-- means for the insured. Where the law hands a judgment to a court or to the insurer's own underwriting (whether I took
-- reasonable care, s 20B(2)-(4); what the insurer would have done without the failure, ss 28(1), 29(1), (3); whether reliance
-- on a term would be a failure of utmost good faith, s 14), the answer is conditional on it, asked as a fact.
--
-- Provisions covered. Act: s 4(1), s 9(1)(a)-(e), (2), s 11(1) ("avoid", "group life contract", "insured", "life insured",
-- "third party beneficiary"), (6), s 11AB(1), (2), s 12, s 13(1), (2A), (3), (4), s 14(1), s 20A, s 20B(1), (5), (6),
-- s 20E, s 21(1)-(3), s 22(1), (3), (5)-(7), s 23A, s 25, s 26(1)-(3), s 27, s 27AA(1), (2), s 28, s 29(1)-(4), (6), (9),
-- (10), s 30(1), (2), s 32(1), s 35(1), (2), s 37, s 40(3), s 43, s 44(2), (3), s 45(1), s 46, s 47, s 53, s 54(1)-(6),
-- s 56(1), s 57(1)-(3), s 58(2), (3), s 59(2), (2A), s 68(2), s 75D(2). Regulations: reg 10 (11%), reg 24 (sickness and accident
-- contracts), reg 26(3) items 1 and 2 (minimum amounts), reg 38 (the interest rate on claims; its formula is not in the deposit).
--
-- Edition: Act -- Compilation No. 32, compilation date 1 March 2024, includes amendments up to Act No. 76, 2023 (register id
-- C2024C00063), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A02944.txt. Regulations -- Compilation No. 3, compilation
-- date 8 October 2026, includes amendments F2026L01225 (register id F2026C01039), as deposited at
-- ../../../insurance-contracts-regulations-2017/registers/source-bundle/F2017L01658.txt.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: ASIC's powers and administration (Part IA, ss 14A, 55A, 75A-75ZE apart from s 75D(2)); insurable interest
-- (Part III); Key Facts Sheets (Division 4 of Part IV); flood (Division 1A of Part V); interim and instalment contracts
-- (ss 38, 39, 62); liability insurance beyond s 40(3) (ss 41, 51); third party beneficiaries' claims (ss 48-48A); s 49, s 50;
-- subrogation (ss 65-68 apart from s 68(2)); cancellation grounds (ss 59A, 60, 61); s 4(2); s 9(1)(f), (1A)-(4); s 22(2);
-- s 27A (split life contracts -- each part is one case here); s 29(5), (7), (8); s 30(3), (3A); s 31 and s 56(2) (the
-- court's power to relieve a fraudulent insured, asked of no one here); s 69, s 71 (notices given late or through a broker);
-- s 75; the other standard covers in regs 15-23, 27-33 and every exclusion in reg 25(2); protected genetic information
-- (reg 13A refers to s 33F of the Act, which is not in the deposited compilation of the Act).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the individual
-- to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test
-- pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Insurance Contracts Act 1984 -- the insured`

§§ `4, 9, 11, 11AB -- my contract`

-- s 4(1): "this Act does not apply to or in relation to a contract of insurance that was entered into before the date of
-- commencement of this Act" (1 January 1986, per the endnotes' commencement column).
-- s 9(1): "this Act does not apply to ... contracts ... (a) of reinsurance; or (b) of insurance entered into ... by a private
-- health insurer ... in respect of its health insurance business ...; or (c) ... by a friendly society; or ... (d) to or in
-- relation to which the Marine Insurance Act 1909 applies; or (e) entered into ... for the purposes of a law ... that relates
-- to: (i) workers' compensation; or (ii) compensation for the death of a person, or for injury to a person, arising out of the
-- use of a motor vehicle". s 9(2): nor to "State insurance or Northern Territory insurance".
DECLARE `What the contract is` IS ONE OF
    `ordinary insurance`
    `reinsurance`
    `health insurance business of a private health insurer`
    `insurance by a friendly society`
    `marine insurance under the Marine Insurance Act 1909`
    `insurance for the purposes of a workers compensation law`
    `insurance for the purposes of a motor vehicle injury compensation law`
    `State or Northern Territory insurance`

-- s 11(6): "a contract of general insurance is a contract of insurance that is not a contract of life insurance"; "contract of
-- life insurance means a contract that constitutes a life policy within the meaning of the Life Insurance Act 1995" (a
-- borrowed term, asked as a fact).
-- s 11AB(1): a consumer insurance contract if "the insurance is obtained wholly or predominantly for the personal, domestic or
-- household purposes of the insured"; (2): or "(a) it is for new business; and (b) the insurer, before the contract is entered
-- into, gives the insured a written notice stating that the contract is a consumer insurance contract".
-- s 11(1): "group life contract means a contract of life insurance that is maintained for the purposes of: (a) a superannuation
-- or retirement scheme under which there can be more than one life insured". For cover in a super fund the insured is the
-- trustee and the member is a life insured.
-- s 11(1): "third party beneficiary ... means a person who is not a party to the contract but is specified or referred to in the
-- contract ... as a person to whom the benefit of the insurance cover provided by the contract extends."
DECLARE `My contract` HAS
    `what it is` IS A `What the contract is`
    `it was entered into before 1 January 1986` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is a life policy` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the insured obtained it wholly or predominantly for personal, domestic or household purposes` IS A BOOLEAN
    `as new business, the insurer said in writing before it was entered into that it is a consumer insurance contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a life insured under it but not the insured` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a party to it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it names or refers to me as someone the cover benefits` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it has a surrender value or covers death` IS A BOOLEAN

@export Does the Insurance Contracts Act apply to my contract (s 4(1), s 9)?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act apply to my contract` IF
        `my contract`'s `what it is` EQUALS `ordinary insurance`
    AND (NOT `my contract`'s `it was entered into before 1 January 1986`)

-- s 11AB(3): in a proceeding the contract "is presumed" to be a consumer insurance contract "unless the contrary is
-- established". The presumption is about proof; here the facts are taken as known.
@export Is my contract a consumer insurance contract (s 11AB)?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my contract a consumer insurance contract` IF
        `my contract`'s `the insured obtained it wholly or predominantly for personal, domestic or household purposes`
    OR  `my contract`'s `as new business, the insurer said in writing before it was entered into that it is a consumer insurance contract`

§§ `12, 20B, 21, 27AA(2) -- what I must do before the contract`

-- s 12: the Act does not impose on an insured "a duty other than: (a) in relation to a consumer insurance contract ... -- the
-- duty to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation; or (b) in relation to any other contract ... -- the duty of
-- disclosure."
-- s 20B(1): "an insured has a duty to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation to the insurer before the relevant
-- contract of insurance is entered into." s 21(1): "an insured has a duty to disclose to the insurer, before the relevant
-- contract of insurance is entered into, every matter" s 21 describes.
-- s 27AA(2): for a contract of life insurance "under which a person other than the insured would become a life insured", the
-- life insured's misrepresentation is a relevant failure if it "would have been a breach of the duty to take reasonable care
-- not to make a misrepresentation if that duty had applied to the life insured ... (whether or not the contract is a consumer
-- insurance contract)". So a member of a super fund is held to the reasonable-care standard, whatever the trustee's contract is.
DECLARE `My duty before the contract` IS ONE OF
    `to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation (s 20B)`
    `to disclose every matter s 21 requires`
    `as a life insured, to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation (s 27AA(2))`
    `none under this Act, which does not apply to the contract`

@export What must I do before my contract of insurance is entered into (s 12, s 20B, s 21, s 27AA(2))?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
GIVETH A `My duty before the contract`
DECIDE `What must I do before the contract is entered into` IS
    BRANCH IF NOT `Does the Act apply to my contract` `my contract`
              THEN `none under this Act, which does not apply to the contract`
           IF `my contract`'s `I am a life insured under it but not the insured`
              THEN `as a life insured, to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation (s 27AA(2))`
           IF `Is my contract a consumer insurance contract` `my contract`
              THEN `to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation (s 20B)`
           OTHERWISE `to disclose every matter s 21 requires`

-- s 21(1): every matter "known to the insured, being a matter that: (a) the insured knows to be a matter relevant to the
-- decision of the insurer whether to accept the risk and, if so, on what terms; or (b) a reasonable person in the circumstances
-- could be expected to know to be a matter so relevant". (2): not a matter "(a) that diminishes the risk; (b) that is of common
-- knowledge; (c) that the insurer knows or in the ordinary course of the insurer's business as an insurer ought to know; or (d)
-- as to which compliance with the duty of disclosure is waived by the insurer." (3): a question left unanswered, or answered in an
-- "obviously incomplete or irrelevant" way, is "deemed" a waiver. s 68(2): the duty "does not require the insured to disclose
-- the existence of a contract that so limits the insured's rights" (to recover damages from someone other than the insurer).
DECLARE `A matter` HAS
    `I know it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I know it is relevant to whether the insurer accepts the risk and on what terms` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a reasonable person in my circumstances could be expected to know it is so relevant` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it diminishes the risk` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is common knowledge` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the insurer knows it or ought to know it in the ordinary course of its business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the insurer waived disclosure of it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the proposal form asked about it and I left it unanswered or gave an obviously incomplete or irrelevant answer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it is an agreement of mine limiting my right to recover damages from someone other than the insurer` IS A BOOLEAN

-- The duty of disclosure is the insured's alone. A life insured who is not the insured (a super fund member) has none: s 25 and
-- s 27AA(2) reach only a life insured's misrepresentation, not silence (INFERENCE from the words "an insured" in s 21(1) and
-- "misrepresentation" in ss 25 and 27AA(2)).
@export Must I disclose this matter to the insurer (s 21, s 68(2))?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
      `the matter` IS A `A matter` @desc The matter and what you know about it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I disclose this matter` IF
        `What must I do before the contract is entered into` `my contract` EQUALS `to disclose every matter s 21 requires`
    AND `the matter`'s `I know it`
    AND (    `the matter`'s `I know it is relevant to whether the insurer accepts the risk and on what terms`
         OR  `the matter`'s `a reasonable person in my circumstances could be expected to know it is so relevant`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `it diminishes the risk`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `it is common knowledge`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `the insurer knows it or ought to know it in the ordinary course of its business`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `the insurer waived disclosure of it`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `the proposal form asked about it and I left it unanswered or gave an obviously incomplete or irrelevant answer`)
    AND (NOT `the matter`'s `it is an agreement of mine limiting my right to recover damages from someone other than the insurer`)

§§ `22 -- what the insurer must tell me, and what happens if it does not`

-- s 22(1): "The insurer must, before a contract of insurance is entered into, clearly inform the insured in writing: (a) of the
-- general nature and effect of the duty of disclosure" and the rest. (3): if it accepts or counter-offers "more than 2 months
-- after the insured's most recent disclosure", it "must give to the insured, with the acceptance or counter-offer, a reminder
-- notice". (5): "An insurer who has not complied with subsection (1) ... may not exercise a right in respect of a failure to
-- comply with the duty of disclosure, unless the failure was fraudulent." (6), (7): without the reminder, no right in respect of
-- a failure about "a new matter", one "of which the insured first becomes aware after the insured's most recent disclosure",
-- "unless the failure was fraudulent". Division 1, and so s 22, applies only to contracts that are not consumer contracts (s 20E).
GIVEN `the day of my most recent disclosure` IS A DATE
      `the day the insurer accepted or counter-offered` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a reminder notice was needed` MEANS
    `the day the insurer accepted or counter-offered` GREATER THAN `add months` `the day of my most recent disclosure` 2

@export May the insurer act on my failure to disclose (s 22(5)-(7))?
GIVEN `the insurer clearly told me in writing of the duty of disclosure before the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the s 22(1) notice was given
      `my failure was fraudulent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the failure to disclose was fraudulent
      `the day of my most recent disclosure` IS A DATE @desc The last day you made disclosure for this contract
      `the day the insurer accepted or counter-offered` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer accepted your offer or counter-offered
      `the insurer sent a reminder notice with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a s 22(3) reminder came with the acceptance
      `I first learned of the matter after my most recent disclosure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is a new matter (s 22(7))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the insurer act on my failure to disclose` IF
        `my failure was fraudulent`
    OR  (    `the insurer clearly told me in writing of the duty of disclosure before the contract`
         AND (NOT (    `a reminder notice was needed` `the day of my most recent disclosure` `the day the insurer accepted or counter-offered`
                   AND (NOT `the insurer sent a reminder notice with it`)
                   AND `I first learned of the matter after my most recent disclosure`)))

§§ `20B(5), (6), 26, 27, 27AA -- misrepresentation and relevant failure`

-- Consumer contracts. s 20B(5): "The insured is not to be taken to have made a misrepresentation merely because the insured: (a)
-- failed to answer a question; or (b) gave an obviously incomplete or irrelevant answer to a question." (6): "a misrepresentation
-- made fraudulently is made in breach of the duty". Whether reasonable care was taken is "determined with regard to all the
-- relevant circumstances" (2), including those listed in (3) and (4); that is a judgment, asked here as a fact.
-- Other contracts. s 26(1): a statement "untrue but ... made on the basis of a belief that the person held, being a belief that a
-- reasonable person in the circumstances would have held ... shall not be taken to be a misrepresentation." (2): nor unless the
-- person "knew, or a reasonable person in the circumstances could be expected to have known, that the statement would have been
-- relevant to the decision of the insurer". (3): s 26 extends to cover for a person "seeking to become a member of a
-- superannuation ... scheme". s 27: no misrepresentation "by reason only that the person failed to answer a question ... or gave
-- an obviously incomplete or irrelevant answer". "Misrepresentation" itself is not defined: an untrue statement (INFERENCE).
DECLARE `A statement I made` HAS
    `it was untrue` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was only a failure to answer, or an obviously incomplete or irrelevant answer` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I believed it, and a reasonable person in my circumstances would have believed it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I knew, or a reasonable person could be expected to have known, it was relevant to the insurer's decision` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I took reasonable care not to make it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was made fraudulently` IS A BOOLEAN

@export Is my statement a misrepresentation (s 20B(5), s 26, s 27)?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
      `my statement` IS A `A statement I made` @desc What you said and what you knew
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my statement a misrepresentation` IF
        `my statement`'s `it was untrue`
    AND (NOT `my statement`'s `it was only a failure to answer, or an obviously incomplete or irrelevant answer`)
    AND (    `Is my contract a consumer insurance contract` `my contract`
         OR  (    (NOT `my statement`'s `I believed it, and a reasonable person in my circumstances would have believed it`)
              AND `my statement`'s `I knew, or a reasonable person could be expected to have known, it was relevant to the insurer's decision`))

-- s 27AA(1): "a relevant failure ... is: (a) if the contract is, or would be, a consumer insurance contract -- a misrepresentation
-- made by the insured in breach of the duty to take reasonable care not to make a misrepresentation; or (b) otherwise: (i) a
-- failure by the insured to comply with the duty of disclosure; or (ii) a misrepresentation made by the insured to the insurer
-- before the contract was entered into." (2): a life insured's misrepresentation, on the reasonable-care standard.
-- s 25: a life insured's misrepresentation has effect "as though the misrepresentation had been so made by the insured".
@export Is my statement a relevant failure that gives the insurer a remedy (s 27AA)?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
      `my statement` IS A `A statement I made` @desc What you said and what you knew
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my statement a relevant failure` IF
        `Does the Act apply to my contract` `my contract`
    AND `Is my statement a misrepresentation` `my contract` `my statement`
    AND (    (    (NOT `Is my contract a consumer insurance contract` `my contract`)
              AND (NOT `my contract`'s `I am a life insured under it but not the insured`))
         OR  (NOT `my statement`'s `I took reasonable care not to make it`)
         OR  `my statement`'s `it was made fraudulently`)

§§ `28, 29, 32 -- what the insurer may do about a relevant failure`

-- s 28(1): s 28 "does not apply if the insurer would have entered into the contract, for the same premium and on the same terms
-- and conditions, even if the failure had not occurred." (2): "If the relevant failure was fraudulent, the insurer may avoid
-- the contract." (3): otherwise "the liability of the insurer in respect of a claim is reduced to the amount that would place
-- the insurer in a position in which the insurer would have been if the relevant failure had not occurred."
-- s 29(1): s 29 does not apply if "(a) the insurer would have entered into the contract even if the failure had not occurred;
-- or (b) the failure was in respect of the date of birth" of a life insured. (2): fraud -- avoid. (3): not fraudulent, and the
-- insurer "would not have been prepared to enter into a contract of life insurance with the insured on any terms" -- it "may,
-- within 3 years after the contract was entered into, avoid the contract." (4): if not avoided, it may by notice "vary the
-- contract by substituting for the sum insured ... a sum that is not less than" the formula sum. (6): if not avoided or varied
-- under (4), it may vary the contract "to place the insurer in the position ... in which the insurer would have been". (10): for
-- "a contract with a surrender value, or a contract that provides insurance cover in respect of the death of a life insured",
-- (4) only "before the expiration of 3 years after the contract was entered into, but not after", and (6) does not apply.
-- s 32(1): for a life insured under a group life contract, as if "the group life contract had been entered into at the time when
-- the proposed life insured became a life insured". "avoid ... means avoid from its inception" (s 11(1)).
-- What the insurer would have done is its underwriting judgment, asked as a fact. "Would have entered into the contract" in
-- s 29(1)(a) is read as the same contract on the same terms (INFERENCE: s 29(4) and (6) would otherwise never be reached).
DECLARE `Without the failure, the insurer would have offered` IS ONE OF
    `the same contract, for the same premium and on the same terms`
    `a contract on other terms or for a higher premium`
    `no contract with me on any terms`

DECLARE `What the insurer may do` IS ONE OF
    `nothing: there was no relevant failure`
    `nothing: it would have entered into the same contract anyway`
    `avoid the contract from the start, because the failure was fraudulent`
    `reduce what it pays on a claim to where it would have been without the failure (s 28(3))`
    `avoid the contract, within 3 years, because it would not have insured me on any terms (s 29(3))`
    `reduce the sum insured to no less than the s 29(4) formula sum, or vary the contract to where it would have been (s 29(6))`
    `reduce the sum insured to no less than the s 29(4) formula sum, and only within 3 years (s 29(10))`
    `nothing: 3 years have passed on cover with a surrender value or for death (s 29(10))`
    `apply the date of birth rules in s 30 instead`

-- "within 3 years after" and "before the expiration of 3 years after" the day the contract was entered into: read as up to and
-- including the third anniversary (INFERENCE; the text does not say whether the anniversary is inside).
GIVEN `entered` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it is within 3 years` MEANS `today` AT MOST `add years` `entered` 3

@export What may the insurer do because of my relevant failure (s 28, s 29, s 32)?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
      `there was a relevant failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there was a relevant failure (s 27AA)
      `it was fraudulent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the failure was fraudulent
      `the insurer would have offered` IS A `Without the failure, the insurer would have offered` @desc What the insurer would have done without the failure
      `it was about a date of birth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the failure was about a life insured's date of birth
      `the day the contract was entered into` IS A DATE @desc For group cover in super, the day you became a life insured (s 32)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day the insurer acts
GIVETH A `What the insurer may do`
DECIDE `What may the insurer do because of my relevant failure` IS
    BRANCH IF NOT `there was a relevant failure`
              THEN `nothing: there was no relevant failure`
           IF `my contract`'s `it is a life policy` AND `it was about a date of birth`
              THEN `apply the date of birth rules in s 30 instead`
           IF `the insurer would have offered` EQUALS `the same contract, for the same premium and on the same terms`
              THEN `nothing: it would have entered into the same contract anyway`
           IF `it was fraudulent`
              THEN `avoid the contract from the start, because the failure was fraudulent`
           IF NOT `my contract`'s `it is a life policy`
              THEN `reduce what it pays on a claim to where it would have been without the failure (s 28(3))`
           IF     `the insurer would have offered` EQUALS `no contract with me on any terms`
              AND `it is within 3 years` `the day the contract was entered into` `today`
              THEN `avoid the contract, within 3 years, because it would not have insured me on any terms (s 29(3))`
           IF `my contract`'s `it has a surrender value or covers death` AND `it is within 3 years` `the day the contract was entered into` `today`
              THEN `reduce the sum insured to no less than the s 29(4) formula sum, and only within 3 years (s 29(10))`
           IF `my contract`'s `it has a surrender value or covers death`
              THEN `nothing: 3 years have passed on cover with a surrender value or for death (s 29(10))`
           OTHERWISE `reduce the sum insured to no less than the s 29(4) formula sum, or vary the contract to where it would have been (s 29(6))`

-- s 29(4): S "is the number of dollars that is equal to the sum insured (including any bonuses)", P "the premium ... or ... the
-- sum of the premiums that have, become payable under the contract", Q "the premium, or ... the sum of the premiums, that the
-- insurer would have been likely to have charged if the relevant failure had not occurred". The formula itself is an image and
-- is NOT in the deposited text. It is taken as S x P / Q, the proportion of cover the premium paid would have bought
-- (INFERENCE from the definitions; to be checked against the printed Act). s 30(1) defines "the standard formula" with the same
-- letters, Q being the premiums on the correct date of birth.
@export What is the least sum insured the insurer may substitute (s 29(4), s 30(1))?
GIVEN `the sum insured` IS A NUMBER @desc S: the sum insured, with any bonuses, in dollars
      `the premiums payable` IS A NUMBER @desc P: the premiums that have become payable, in dollars
      `the premiums the insurer would have charged` IS A NUMBER @desc Q: the premiums the insurer would likely have charged, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the least sum insured the insurer may substitute` IS
    `the sum insured` TIMES `the premiums payable` DIVIDED BY `the premiums the insurer would have charged`

§§ `30 -- a misstated date of birth`

-- s 30(2): "(a) where the sum insured ... exceeds the amount ... ascertained in accordance with the standard formula -- the
-- insurer may at any time vary the contract by substituting ... an amount that is not less than the amount ... so ascertained;
-- and (b) where the sum insured ... is less than the amount so ascertained, the insurer shall either: (i) reduce ... the premium
-- ... and repay the amount of overpayments of premium ... together with interest on that amount at the prescribed rate ...; or
-- (ii) vary the contract by substituting ... the amount ... so ascertained."
-- Reg 10: "For the purposes of subparagraph 30(2)(b)(i) of the Act, the rate of 11% per annum is prescribed."
DECLARE `What the date of birth rules give` IS ONE OF
    `the insurer may reduce the sum insured to no less than the standard formula sum, at any time`
    `the insurer must refund overpaid premium with interest at 11% a year, or raise the sum insured to the standard formula sum`
    `nothing changes: the sum insured equals the standard formula sum`

@export What happens because my date of birth was misstated (s 30(2), reg 10)?
GIVEN `the sum insured` IS A NUMBER @desc S: the sum insured, with any bonuses, in dollars
      `the premiums payable` IS A NUMBER @desc P: the premiums that have become payable, in dollars
      `the premiums on my correct date of birth` IS A NUMBER @desc Q: the premiums that would have been payable on the correct date of birth
GIVETH A `What the date of birth rules give`
DECIDE `What happens because my date of birth was misstated` IS
    BRANCH IF `the sum insured` GREATER THAN `What is the least sum insured the insurer may substitute` `the sum insured` `the premiums payable` `the premiums on my correct date of birth`
              THEN `the insurer may reduce the sum insured to no less than the standard formula sum, at any time`
           IF `the sum insured` LESS THAN `What is the least sum insured the insurer may substitute` `the sum insured` `the premiums payable` `the premiums on my correct date of birth`
              THEN `the insurer must refund overpaid premium with interest at 11% a year, or raise the sum insured to the standard formula sum`
           OTHERWISE `nothing changes: the sum insured equals the standard formula sum`

§§ `13, 75D(2) -- the duty of the utmost good faith`

-- s 13(1): "there is implied in such a contract a provision requiring each party to it to act towards the other party, in
-- respect of any matter arising under or in relation to it, with the utmost good faith." (3): "a party ... includes ... a third
-- party beneficiary under the contract." (4): but for a third party beneficiary "only after the contract is entered into".
-- The duty runs both ways; the two questions below have the same answer by design.
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract`
      `the contract has been entered into` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the utmost good faith binds the insurer and me` MEANS
        `Does the Act apply to my contract` `my contract`
    AND (    `my contract`'s `I am a party to it`
         OR  (`my contract`'s `it names or refers to me as someone the cover benefits` AND `the contract has been entered into`))

@export Must my insurer act towards me with the utmost good faith (s 13)?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
      `the contract has been entered into` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract has been entered into yet
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my insurer act towards me with the utmost good faith` IF
    `the utmost good faith binds the insurer and me` `my contract` `the contract has been entered into`

@export Must I act towards my insurer with the utmost good faith (s 13)?
GIVEN `my contract` IS A `My contract` @desc Your contract of insurance
      `the contract has been entered into` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract has been entered into yet
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I act towards my insurer with the utmost good faith` IF
    `the utmost good faith binds the insurer and me` `my contract` `the contract has been entered into`

-- s 13(2A): an insurer that fails to comply with the implied provision contravenes the subsection. "Civil penalty: 5,000 penalty
-- units." s 75D(2): for a body corporate "the greatest of: (a) the penalty specified ..., multiplied by 10; and (b) if the court
-- can determine the benefit derived and detriment avoided ... -- that amount multiplied by 3; and (c) either: (i) 10% of the
-- annual turnover ...; or (ii) if the amount worked out under subparagraph (i) is greater than ... 2.5 million penalty units --
-- 2.5 million penalty units." The value of a penalty unit is set by the Crimes Act 1914, not here: amounts are in penalty units.
@export What is the most my insurer can be ordered to pay for breaching the utmost good faith, in penalty units (s 13(2A), s 75D(2))?
GIVEN `the benefit derived and detriment avoided, in penalty units` IS A NUMBER @desc 0 if the court cannot determine it
      `10% of the insurer's annual turnover, in penalty units` IS A NUMBER @desc For the 12 months ending with the month of the breach
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most my insurer can be ordered to pay` IS
    max (5000 TIMES 10)
        (max (`the benefit derived and detriment avoided, in penalty units` TIMES 3)
             (min `10% of the insurer's annual turnover, in penalty units` 2500000))

§§ `14, 37, 43, 44, 45, 46, 47, 53 -- terms the insurer may not rely on`

-- s 14(1): "If reliance by a party ... on a provision of the contract would be to fail to act with the utmost good faith, the party
-- may not rely on the provision." (a court's judgment, asked as a fact)
-- s 37: no reliance on a provision "of a kind that is not usually included in contracts of insurance that provide similar
-- insurance cover unless, before the contract was entered into the insurer clearly informed the insured in writing of the
-- effect of the provision". s 44(1): no reliance on an average provision unless "the insurer clearly informed the insured in
-- writing of the nature and effect of the provision". s 43(1): an arbitration provision "is void"; (2): unless the agreement
-- "was made after the dispute or difference arose". s 45(1): a provision limiting liability because the insured "has entered
-- into some other contract of insurance" (not one a law requires) "is void". s 46(2), s 47(2): where the insured "was not aware
-- of, and a reasonable person in the circumstances could not be expected to have been aware of" the defect, sickness or
-- disability, the insurer "may not rely on" a pre-contract-condition exclusion. s 53: a provision that "authorizes or permits
-- the insurer to vary, to the prejudice of a person other than the insurer, the contract ... is void" (outside classes the
-- regulations exclude, reg 37: not encoded).
DECLARE `What kind of term` IS ONE OF
    `an ordinary term`
    `an unusual term`
    `an average provision`
    `an arbitration clause`
    `an other-insurance clause`
    `a pre-existing defect exclusion`
    `a pre-existing sickness or disability exclusion`
    `a term letting the insurer vary the contract to my prejudice`

DECLARE `A term of my policy` HAS
    `kind` IS A `What kind of term`
    `the insurer clearly told me in writing of its effect before the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `when the contract was made I knew, or a reasonable person would have known, of the defect, sickness or disability` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I agreed to arbitration after the dispute arose` IS A BOOLEAN
    `relying on it would be to fail to act with the utmost good faith` IS A BOOLEAN

@export May the insurer rely on this term of my policy against me (ss 14, 37, 43-47, 53)?
GIVEN `the term` IS A `A term of my policy` @desc The term and how it was put to you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the insurer rely on this term against me` IF
        (NOT `the term`'s `relying on it would be to fail to act with the utmost good faith`)
    AND CONSIDER `the term`'s `kind`
        WHEN `an ordinary term` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an unusual term` THEN `the term`'s `the insurer clearly told me in writing of its effect before the contract`
        WHEN `an average provision` THEN `the term`'s `the insurer clearly told me in writing of its effect before the contract`
        WHEN `an arbitration clause` THEN `the term`'s `I agreed to arbitration after the dispute arose`
        WHEN `an other-insurance clause` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `a pre-existing defect exclusion` THEN `the term`'s `when the contract was made I knew, or a reasonable person would have known, of the defect, sickness or disability`
        WHEN `a pre-existing sickness or disability exclusion` THEN `the term`'s `when the contract was made I knew, or a reasonable person would have known, of the defect, sickness or disability`
        WHEN `a term letting the insurer vary the contract to my prejudice` THEN FALSE

-- s 44(2): for a home building or its contents, where the sum insured "is not less than 80% of the value of the property, the
-- liability of the insurer ... is not reduced by reason only of the operation of an average provision". (3): below 80%, an
-- average provision cannot reduce liability below the formula amount, where "A is ... the amount of the loss or damage. S is ...
-- the sum insured ... P is 80% of ... the value of the property." The formula is an image NOT in the deposit; taken as A x S / P
-- (INFERENCE from the definitions). The result is never more than the loss, and never more than the sum insured (INFERENCE: the
-- average provision only ever reduces).
@export What is the least my home insurer may pay me despite an average provision (s 44(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the loss` IS A NUMBER @desc A: the amount of the loss or damage, in dollars
      `the sum insured` IS A NUMBER @desc S: the sum insured on the property, in dollars
      `the value of the property` IS A NUMBER @desc Its indemnity or replacement value when the contract was entered into
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the least my home insurer may pay me despite an average provision` IS
    IF `the sum insured` AT LEAST (0.8 TIMES `the value of the property`)
    THEN min `the loss` `the sum insured`
    ELSE min (min `the loss` `the sum insured`) (`the loss` TIMES `the sum insured` DIVIDED BY (0.8 TIMES `the value of the property`))

§§ `54 -- something done after the contract`

-- s 54(1): where the contract would let the insurer refuse a claim "by reason of some act of the insured or of some other person,
-- being an act that occurred after the contract was entered into", it "may not refuse to pay the claim by reason only of that
-- act but the insurer's liability in respect of the claim is reduced by the amount that fairly represents the extent to which
-- the insurer's interests were prejudiced as a result of that act." (2): it may refuse "where the act could reasonably be
-- regarded as being capable of causing or contributing to a loss in respect of which insurance cover is provided". (3): not if
-- "the insured proves that no part of the loss ... was caused by the act"; (4): not for the part the insured proves "was not
-- caused by the act". (5): not where "(a) the act was necessary to protect the safety of a person or to preserve property; or (b)
-- it was not reasonably possible for the insured or other person not to do the act". (6): an act "includes ... an omission".
-- A late claim, or a claim made after a time limit in the policy, is an omission after the contract (INFERENCE from (6)); the Act
-- sets no general time for making a claim.
-- Where (5) applies, the insurer is still left with the (1) reduction for prejudice (INFERENCE: (5) speaks only of refusing).
DECLARE `Something done after the contract` HAS
    `the policy would let the insurer refuse my claim because of it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it could reasonably be regarded as capable of causing or contributing to a loss the policy covers` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have proved no part of the loss was caused by it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have proved some part of the loss was not caused by it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was necessary to protect someone's safety or to preserve property` IS A BOOLEAN
    `it was not reasonably possible not to do it` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Whether the insurer may refuse` IS ONE OF
    `s 54 does not apply: the policy does not let the insurer refuse for this`
    `no: it may only reduce the claim by the prejudice it suffered (s 54(1))`
    `yes: the act could cause or contribute to a covered loss (s 54(2))`
    `no: I proved no part of the loss was caused by the act (s 54(3))`
    `only for the part of the loss the act caused (s 54(4))`
    `no: the act was needed for safety or to preserve property, or could not reasonably be avoided (s 54(5))`

@export May the insurer refuse my claim because of something done after the contract (s 54)?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `Something done after the contract` @desc The act or omission and what you can prove
GIVETH A `Whether the insurer may refuse`
DECIDE `May the insurer refuse my claim because of what happened after the contract` IS
    BRANCH IF NOT `what happened`'s `the policy would let the insurer refuse my claim because of it`
              THEN `s 54 does not apply: the policy does not let the insurer refuse for this`
           IF     `what happened`'s `it was necessary to protect someone's safety or to preserve property`
              OR  `what happened`'s `it was not reasonably possible not to do it`
              THEN `no: the act was needed for safety or to preserve property, or could not reasonably be avoided (s 54(5))`
           IF NOT `what happened`'s `it could reasonably be regarded as capable of causing or contributing to a loss the policy covers`
              THEN `no: it may only reduce the claim by the prejudice it suffered (s 54(1))`
           IF `what happened`'s `I have proved no part of the loss was caused by it`
              THEN `no: I proved no part of the loss was caused by the act (s 54(3))`
           IF `what happened`'s `I have proved some part of the loss was not caused by it`
              THEN `only for the part of the loss the act caused (s 54(4))`
           OTHERWISE `yes: the act could cause or contribute to a covered loss (s 54(2))`

@export How much must the insurer still pay on my claim, after the reduction for prejudice (s 54(1))?
GIVEN `my claim` IS A NUMBER @desc What the insurer would otherwise pay, in dollars
      `the prejudice the insurer suffered` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount that fairly represents the prejudice to the insurer, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much must the insurer still pay on my claim` IS
    max 0 (`my claim` MINUS `the prejudice the insurer suffered`)

§§ `40(3), 56, 57 -- making the claim, and interest`

-- s 40(1), (3): under a claims-made liability policy, "Where the insured gave notice in writing to the insurer of facts that might
-- give rise to a claim against the insured as soon as was reasonably practicable after the insured became aware of those facts
-- but before the insurance cover provided by the contract expired, the insurer is not relieved of liability ... by reason only
-- that it was made after the expiration of the period of the insurance cover".
@export Does my liability insurer still cover a claim made against me after the cover ended (s 40(3))?
GIVEN `the policy excludes claims notified after the cover ends` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the policy is a claims-made policy (s 40(1))
      `I gave written notice of the facts as soon as reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc After you became aware of the facts
      `I gave that notice before the cover ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the notice was given during the period of cover
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my liability insurer still cover the late claim` IF
        (NOT `the policy excludes claims notified after the cover ends`)
    OR  (`I gave written notice of the facts as soon as reasonably practicable` AND `I gave that notice before the cover ended`)

-- s 56(1): "Where a claim ... is made fraudulently, the insurer may not avoid the contract but may refuse payment of the claim."
-- (2): a court may order payment where "only a minimal or insignificant part of the claim is made fraudulently and non-payment of
-- the remainder ... would be harsh and unfair" (a court's judgment, asked as a fact).
DECLARE `What the insurer may do about my claim` IS ONE OF
    `pay it: it was not made fraudulently`
    `refuse it, but not avoid the contract (s 56(1))`
    `pay what the court orders, though part was fraudulent (s 56(2))`

@export What may the insurer do about my claim if part of it was fraudulent (s 56)?
GIVEN `the claim was made fraudulently` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of the claim was made fraudulently
      `a court has ordered payment under s 56(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court found the fraud minimal and refusal harsh and unfair
GIVETH A `What the insurer may do about my claim`
DECIDE `What may the insurer do about my claim` IS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the claim was made fraudulently` THEN `pay it: it was not made fraudulently`
           IF `a court has ordered payment under s 56(2)` THEN `pay what the court orders, though part was fraudulent (s 56(2))`
           OTHERWISE `refuse it, but not avoid the contract (s 56(1))`

-- s 57(1): the insurer "is also liable to pay interest on the amount". (2): for "the period commencing on the day as from which it
-- was unreasonable for the insurer to have withheld payment ... and ending on whichever is the earlier of ... (a) the day on
-- which the payment is made; (b) the day on which the payment is sent by post". (3): at "the rate ... prescribed by, or worked
-- out in a manner prescribed by, the regulations". Reg 38(1) works it out from Y, the 10-year Australian Government Bond yield at
-- the end of the relevant half-financial year(s); the formula itself is an image NOT in the deposit, so the rate is an input.
-- The period counts both its first and last day; interest is simple, at the yearly rate over 365 days (INFERENCE on both).
@export How much interest must the insurer pay me on a claim it withheld (s 57, reg 38)?
GIVEN `the amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount the insurer was liable to pay, in dollars
      `the reg 38 rate, per cent a year` IS A NUMBER @desc The rate worked out under reg 38
      `the day it became unreasonable to withhold payment` IS A DATE @desc The first day of the interest period
      `the day the payment was made or posted, whichever is earlier` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the interest period
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much interest must the insurer pay me` IS
    `the amount` TIMES `the reg 38 rate, per cent a year` DIVIDED BY 100
        TIMES (Day `the day the payment was made or posted, whichever is earlier` MINUS Day `the day it became unreasonable to withhold payment` PLUS 1)
        DIVIDED BY 365

§§ `58, 59 -- renewal and cancellation`

-- s 58(2): "Not later than 14 days before the day on which renewable insurance cover provided under a contract of general
-- insurance ... expires, the insurer shall give to the insured ... a notice in writing" of the expiry and whether it will
-- negotiate to renew. (3): if it does not, and "before the original contract expired, the insured had not obtained from some
-- other insurer insurance cover to replace" it, "there exists between the parties ... a contract of insurance that provides
-- insurance cover as provided by the original contract" for a further period. (4): no premium unless a claim is made.
@export Does my general insurance cover carry on because the insurer did not warn me it was expiring (s 58)?
GIVEN `it is renewable general insurance cover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Cover for a period, of a kind usually renewed (s 58(1))
      `the day the cover expires` IS A DATE @desc The expiry day of the cover
      `the insurer gave the expiry notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a written expiry notice was given at all
      `the day the notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the expiry notice was given (any day if none)
      `I had replacement cover from another insurer before expiry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you replaced the cover in time
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my cover carry on` IF
        `it is renewable general insurance cover`
    AND (NOT (`the insurer gave the expiry notice` AND `the day the notice was given` AT MOST (`the day the cover expires` MINUS 14)))
    AND (NOT `I had replacement cover from another insurer before expiry`)

-- s 59(1): an insurer wishing to cancel "shall give notice in writing". (2), (2A): it takes effect at the earlier of replacement
-- cover and the latest of "4 pm on the applicable business day", a time in the contract, a time in the notice; "applicable
-- business day means: (a) in respect of a contract that is not a contract of life insurance: (i) if the contract is in force
-- because of section 58 -- the fourteenth business day; or (ii) otherwise -- the third business day; or (b) in respect of a
-- contract of life insurance -- the twentieth business day; after the day on which the notice was given".
@export On which business day after the notice can a cancellation of my contract take effect, at 4 pm, at the earliest (s 59(2A))?
GIVEN `it is a contract of life insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contract is a life policy
      `it is in force because of s 58` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cover is carrying on under s 58(3)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `On which business day can a cancellation take effect` IS
    BRANCH IF `it is a contract of life insurance` THEN 20
           IF `it is in force because of s 58` THEN 14
           OTHERWISE 3

§§ `35, regs 24, 26 -- standard cover for sickness and accident`

-- Reg 24(1): sickness and accident contracts are a class to which Division 1 of Part V (standard cover) applies -- contracts that
-- cover the insured person "(a) contracting a sickness or disease ...; or (b) sustaining an injury ... where the insured or one of
-- the insureds is a natural person." (2): "the class of contracts does not include ... (a) a life policy within the meaning of
-- the Life Insurance Act 1995; (b) a continuous disability insurance contract incorporated within a life policy ...; (c) a
-- sickness and accident policy that: (i) is guaranteed renewable at the option of the insured; or (ii) the insurer guarantees not
-- to cancel in response to a change in the risk; where the policy has been effected for a predetermined period of years in
-- excess of 1 year".
@export Does standard cover apply to my sickness or injury policy (reg 24)?
GIVEN `it covers me for sickness or injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the policy covers sickness, disease or injury
      `an insured is a natural person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or another insured, is an individual
      `it is a life policy, or a continuous disability cover inside one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For example income protection held through a super fund's life policy
      `it is guaranteed renewable or non-cancellable for a fixed term over 1 year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Reg 24(2)(c)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does standard cover apply to my sickness or injury policy` IF
        `it covers me for sickness or injury`
    AND `an insured is a natural person`
    AND (NOT `it is a life policy, or a continuous disability cover inside one`)
    AND (NOT `it is guaranteed renewable or non-cancellable for a fixed term over 1 year`)

-- Reg 26(3): the minimum amount is the amount in column 2, "reduced by an amount equal to the amount so payable in respect of the
-- first 14 days of the period during which the insured person is disabled". Item 1 (death): "The amount specified in the contract
-- ... or, if no amount is so specified, $25,000". Item 2 (total disablement): "(a) [if] a daily amount is specified ... that
-- amount multiplied by the number of days during which the insured person is so disabled". There is no disablement period on a
-- death claim, so nothing is deducted from item 1 (INFERENCE). A daily amount of 0 here means none is specified; item 2(b), lost
-- income, is not encoded.
-- s 35(1): the insurer "may not refuse to pay an amount equal to the minimum amount" on a prescribed event, unless (2) it proves it
-- "clearly informed the insured in writing" of the lesser cover, or the insured "knew, or a reasonable person ... could be
-- expected to have known". In that case the answer is 0: s 35 then sets no minimum at all.
@export What is the minimum the insurer must pay on my death claim under standard cover (s 35, reg 26(3) item 1)?
GIVEN `the death benefit stated in the contract` IS A NUMBER @desc In dollars; 0 if none is stated
      `the insurer clearly told me in writing of the lesser cover, or I knew of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 35(2)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the minimum on a death claim` IS
    BRANCH IF `the insurer clearly told me in writing of the lesser cover, or I knew of it` THEN 0
           IF `the death benefit stated in the contract` GREATER THAN 0 THEN `the death benefit stated in the contract`
           OTHERWISE 25000

@export What is the minimum the insurer must pay on my total disablement claim under standard cover (s 35, reg 26(3) item 2(a))?
GIVEN `the daily amount for total disablement` IS A NUMBER @desc The daily amount specified in the contract, in dollars
      `the days I was totally disabled` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days of total disablement
      `the insurer clearly told me in writing of the lesser cover, or I knew of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc s 35(2)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the minimum on a total disablement claim` IS
    IF `the insurer clearly told me in writing of the lesser cover, or I knew of it` THEN 0
    ELSE `the daily amount for total disablement` TIMES (max 0 (`the days I was totally disabled` MINUS 14))
